course: dictionary
lesson: 7937
title: Reap: gathering crops and getting results
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Reap is a verb, so it is a doing word. At first it means to cut and gather a crop, like wheat or corn, from a field. «Farmers reap the corn in late summer.» The clues are farmers, corn and late summer, so reap must mean to cut the crop when it is ready.
explain: Reap also means to get something as a result of what you have done. «She worked hard all term and will reap the rewards.» Rewards are good things you get back, so reap the rewards means you get good results from your effort. Swap your meaning into the sentence: «She worked hard all term and will get the rewards» still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Sometimes reap is part of a famous saying: «You reap what you sow.» Sow means to plant seeds, so the saying is about planting and harvesting. It means what you do now decides what will happen to you later. Words that go together like this are called phrases, and they often have a meaning that is different from the single words.
fact: Reap means to cut and gather a crop, like wheat or corn.
fact: «Reap the rewards» and «reap the benefits» mean to get good things as a result of what you have done.
fact: «You reap what you sow» means what you do now decides what will happen to you later (Galatians 6:7).
fact: Long ago, reaping was done by hand with sickles and scythes. It was harvest work.
fact: A harvest festival gives thanks for what has been reaped.
exercise: «Farmers reap the corn in late summer.» What does reap mean here? | Plant seeds in spring | *To cut and gather a crop | Sell animals at the market | Paint the barn red
exercise: «She worked hard all term and will reap the rewards.» What does reap the rewards mean? | *Get good things as a result of her effort | Lose her books | Break the rules | Take a long holiday
exercise: The saying «You reap what you sow» means what? | Eating fruit from the garden | Only farmers can use this saying | Seeds grow faster in the dark | *What you do now decides what will happen to you later
test: «Long ago, people reaped the wheat with sickles and scythes.» What were sickles and scythes used for? | Mending roofs | *Cutting crops at harvest | Sailing boats | Digging wells
test: At a harvest festival, what do people give thanks for? | Christmas presents | Summer holidays at the seaside | *What has been reaped | The first snow of winter
exam: In the verse «A man reaps what he sows» (Galatians 6:7), what does reap mean? | To thank the farmer for the harvest | To cut wheat in a field | To get lots of money quickly | *What you do now decides what will happen to you later
